# Example de-identification recipe for medimage/dicom-series.
#
# This is a pydicom `deid` RECIPE, not JSON, and the path is the format name.
# xnat-ingest's load_specs walks <spec-dir>/<category>/<format>, so the DIRECTORY
# is the category and the FILE is the format: medimage/dicom-series. It skips
# anything at the top level that is not a directory, so a flat file named
# `medimage@dicom-series.json` is never read and every session fails with
# "No deidentification specs found".
#
# Copy this directory, rename __default__ to your project ID for a
# project-specific policy, and edit to match your site's approved profile.
# __default__ is the fallback for any project without its own directory.
#
# ONLY THE TAGS NAMED HERE ARE REMOVED. Anything not listed passes through
# untouched, so this list IS the policy — review it as such.
#
# Compare with files/deidentification-profile.example.json, which is the
# equivalent for the Orthanc Lua engine and has a different syntax.

FORMAT dicom

%header

# Direct identifiers.
REMOVE PatientName
REMOVE PatientBirthDate
REMOVE PatientAddress
REMOVE PatientTelephoneNumbers
REMOVE OtherPatientIDs
REMOVE OtherPatientNames

# Care-team and institution.
REMOVE ReferringPhysicianName
REMOVE PerformingPhysicianName
REMOVE OperatorsName
REMOVE InstitutionName
REMOVE InstitutionAddress

# Local accession/order numbers, which are re-identifying within a site.
REMOVE AccessionNumber

# THE PATIENT IDENTIFIER. Removed, and the reason matters because an earlier
# version of this file kept it on a premise that is false.
#
# It claimed assign had already rewritten PatientID to the routed subject.
# assign does no such thing: it READS tag values to resolve the project,
# subject and session ids and then copies or hardlinks the files unchanged. It
# never opens a dataset for writing. Verified by grepping the package for any
# write to PatientID; there is none.
#
# It also claimed upload needs the tag to place the session. It does not.
# Placement is explicit: upload builds the XNAT path from the session object's
# ids, which came from ingest.assign.tagMapping, not from this header. So
# removing PatientID cannot detach the data from its subject.
#
# What DOES happen if it is left: upload finishes by calling
# /data/experiments/<id>?pullDataFromHeaders=true, which asks XNAT to populate
# its own metadata FROM THESE HEADERS. So a real medical record number left
# here is not merely present in the pixel archive, it is pulled into XNAT's
# fields, while PatientName, birth date and institution are all correctly
# stripped and the result looks de-identified to a spot check.
#
# Measured before this change: an instance de-identified by this recipe and
# uploaded still carried PatientID verbatim, with PatientIdentityRemoved absent.
REMOVE PatientID

# QUASI-IDENTIFIERS, left in deliberately but LISTED so the choice is visible.
# Age and sex are research-relevant and are not direct identifiers, but in a
# small cohort they narrow it sharply. Decide per site; they are named here
# rather than omitted so that deciding is not the same as forgetting.
#   PatientSex
#   PatientAge
